<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sharof Rashidov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1983">1983</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sharaf Rashidov</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sharof Rashidov was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> writer and politician who led the Communist Party of the Uzbek SSR as first secretary from 1959 until his death in 1983. He built a powerful patronage network and promised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> ever larger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> harvests. After his death investigations exposed the 'cotton scandal', a vast fraud in which billions of roubles had been paid for cotton that was never produced.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sharof Rashidov was born on 6 November 1917, the day before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a>, into a poor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> peasant family in the village of Sovungarlik, in what is now the Jizzakh region. After the Jizzakh Pedagogical College in 1935 he taught at a secondary school. He graduated in philology from Samarkand State University in 1941, and that year he was sent to the Eastern Front as a junior lieutenant. He fought in the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, received two Orders of the Red Star and was demobilised in 1943 after being severely wounded. He also wrote, and his epic Chegarachi appeared in 1937.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Rashidov sat in the Supreme Soviet of the Uzbek SSR from 1947, chaired its Presidium from 1950 to 1959, and in March 1959 became First Secretary of the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, a post he held until his death. He led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> faction and purged Tashkent-aligned politicians. In 1969 rivals from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>, Fergana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> factions tried to depose him; he prevailed, and the plotters lost their power.</p><p>He enjoyed the support of Brezhnev, who sent resources for industrialisation and shielded him from investigation, and the republic had an unusually high degree of autonomy. Critics likened him to a khan for his control, and he kept a network of informers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> saw rapid growth, with the Muruntau gold mine opening in 1969 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> rebuilt after the earthquake of 1966.</p><p>He promoted relatives to office: at least fourteen held important government posts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> demanded ever more <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, and the republic's government reported inflated figures for irrigated and harvested land and output. Bribes, tribute and forgery created the appearance of fulfilled quotas, and wealth from central Soviet funds passed to the republic and to the Rashidov family. The push for cotton also contributed to the shrinking of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aral-sea">Aral Sea</a>. Yuri Andropov, who knew of the scheme, began to break up the cotton mafia after Brezhnev's death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rashidov died on 31 October 1983 while visiting the Karakalpak ASSR. Most sources indicate a heart attack, with the strain of the corruption investigation often suggested as a factor, but some accounts allege suicide. Inomjon Usmonxoʻjayev succeeded him. He was first buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>'s Lenin Square, but after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> scandal drew attention his remains were disinterred in 1986 and reburied in his home district. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> independence <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam-karimov">Islam Karimov</a> rehabilitated his image, a district was named after him, and a museum in Jizzakh is dedicated to him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sharof Rashidov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Sharof Rashidov</b>, first secretary from 1959 to 1983, the 'cotton scandal' exposed massive fraud.</blockquote><a href="/state/uzbek-ssr">Uzbek SSR</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jizzakh, in what is now <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.12,67.85&amp;year=1959" aria-label="Jizzakh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Sharof Rashidov"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5512 4122h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5512 4122h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.12,67.85&amp;year=1959">What was here in 1959? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
